<Guide
  title="Med Spa SEO in Dallas–Fort Worth: Google, Maps & AI Search — Azoth & Co."
  description="How patients in McKinney, Frisco, Plano and across DFW choose a med spa now, and the fixes that move a practice into the map pack and AI answers. Start with a $2,500 Visibility Audit."
  label="Med spas"
  heading="Search visibility for med spas in Dallas–Fort Worth."
  sub="Patients choose from the map, the first three results and the AI answer. Most practices lose them at one of those three."
  published="2026-11-11"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    A patient looking for Botox in Frisco or a facial in McKinney rarely scrolls. She looks at the map, opens two or
    three profiles, reads the newest reviews, glances at photos and books. More and more she asks ChatGPT or reads
    Google’s AI answer first. The practice that is not in that first screen is not in the decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    North Texas has no shortage of med spas, and the best one in town is not always the one that gets found. Here is
    where we see practices lose patients, and what fixes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where med spas lose patients</h2>
  <h3>The wrong category, or too few</h3>
  <p>
    “Medical spa” as the primary category, with the secondary categories that match what you really do: skin care
    clinic, laser hair removal service, and so on. A practice listed as a generic beauty business competes in the wrong
    search.
  </p>
  <h3>One page for every treatment</h3>
  <p>
    Patients search “lip filler Plano”, “HydraFacial Allen”, “laser hair removal McKinney”. A site with a single services
    list ranks for none of those. Each core treatment needs its own page: what it is, who it suits, how long it takes,
    what it costs or how it is priced, downtime, and the towns you serve.
  </p>
  <h3>Reviews that stopped</h3>
  <p>
    Patients read the newest reviews first. Fifty reviews from two years ago lose to twenty from this season. The fix is
    a habit: ask on the day of the visit, with a direct link, and reply to every review.
  </p>
  <h3>Booking friction</h3>
  <p>
    A profile without a booking link, or a site where booking takes five taps on a phone, loses the patient who is ready
    now. The booking button belongs on the profile and on every treatment page.
  </p>
  <h3>Nothing for AI to quote</h3>
  <p>
    AI assistants recommend practices they can confirm across sources. Clear treatment pages, consistent listings and
    recent, specific reviews are what they read. Our guide to <a href="/visibility/ai-search/">AI search</a> covers it
    in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Med spa rules to respect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use only your own before-and-after photos, with written patient consent.</li>
    <li>Do not promise results. Describe the treatment and who it suits.</li>
    <li>Never offer discounts or gifts in exchange for reviews.</li>
    <li>Keep patient details out of review replies. Thank them; do not confirm they are a patient.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the Visibility Audit covers for a med spa</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your map position for your top treatments, across the towns you draw from</li>
    <li>Your Google profile against the three practices that show up instead of you</li>
    <li>Treatment pages, speed on a phone, and booking flow</li>
    <li>What ChatGPT, Perplexity and Google’s AI answer say when a patient asks for a med spa in your town</li>
    <li>A ranked fix list with 90-day targets</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The med spas that show up instead of you add reviews and pages every week, so the gap widens while it waits.
    <a href="/visibility/#audit">The Audit</a> shows where you stand and what to fix first.
  </p>
</Guide>
